  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/identcpu.c:210-236  parse_ext_z
      The Z-extension ISA string parser only recognizes zicbom, zicboz, and zicbop. The 'zacas' extension string is not recognized, meaning the kernel has zero awareness of whether the hardware supports AMOCAS instructions. This is a silent-extension-enablement problem: the hardware may execute amocas.w/d/q in userspace while the kernel has no flag to track it, no ability to block it if the hardware has the deferred-write-check erratum, and no mechanism to apply mitigations.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/identcpu.c:444-476  update_global_capabilities
      The function propagates only zicbom/zicboz/zicbop to global capability flags and AT_HWCAP. No zacas capability bit exists. The HWCAP_ISA_A bit (set from single-letter 'a' in the ISA string) only indicates the base Zaamo/Zalrsc atomics — not Zacas. Userspace can execute AMOCAS on hardware that supports it, but the kernel never advertises this to userspace via any standard mechanism, and never applies any zacas-specific defenses.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/trap.c:256-262  page_fault_handler
      SCAUSE_STORE_PAGE_FAULT is unconditionally mapped to VM_PROT_WRITE (ftype = VM_PROT_WRITE). For spec-compliant hardware, this is correct: AMOCAS on a read-only page always faults before comparison, so the attacker gets no oracle. But for hardware with the deferred-check erratum, the AMOCAS instruction faults only when comparison succeeds (write attempted) and silently reads (no fault) when comparison fails. The kernel treats this as an ordinary write fault and delivers SIGSEGV — turning the fault into an oracle: page fault = value matched. The kernel has no mitigation (e.g., mandatory zacas trapping, per-page tracking, or explicit AMOCAS-emulation on suspect pages).
  [OK] sys/riscv/riscv/pmap.c:2932-2936  pmap_fault
      pmap_fault correctly checks PTE_W for write faults, PTE_X for execute faults, and PTE_R for read faults. For a genuinely read-only page, the check 'ftype == VM_PROT_WRITE && (oldpte & PTE_W) == 0' correctly returns 0 (no spurious-fault fixup), causing the normal vm_fault_trap path to be followed. This is spec-compliant at the software level: the fault handler will correctly refuse to make a read-only page writable. The vulnerability, if present, lies in the hardware's deferral of the permission check, not in this software path.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/db_disasm.c:162-183  (instruction table)
      The disassembler table includes AMOADD, AMOXOR, AMOOR, AMOAND, AMOMIN, AMOMAX, AMOMINU, AMOMAXU, AMOSWAP (both .w and .d), plus LR/SC — but no amocas.w, amocas.d, or amocas.q entries. When an AMOCAS instruction causes a trap, the kernel's dump_regs output will show the raw opcode bytes but won't decode the instruction mnemonic, making debugging and forensics harder. More critically, the absence of AMOCAS opcode definitions means no kernel code path can pattern-match on AMOCAS instructions during trap handling — for example, a trap handler cannot distinguish AMOCAS from other store-type faults to apply potential mitigations.
